    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Gauss and the class"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A teacher once asked a class to add 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100. A boy named Gauss answered in seconds. He paired 1 with 100, 2 with 99 and so on - fifty pairs each making 101."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "series",
          "S_n = n/2[2a + (n-1)d]",
          "S_n = n/2(a + l)",
          "5050",
          "n(n+1)/2",
          "odd numbers: n^2",
          "t_n = S_n - S_(n-1)"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "A series is the sum of the terms of a sequence, for example 1 + 3 + 5 + 7.\nFor an AP with first term a, common difference d and n terms, the sum is S_n = n/2 x [2a + (n - 1)d].\nIf the last term l is known, S_n = n/2 x (a + l).\nCheck with Gauss: n = 100, a = 1, l = 100, so S = 100/2 x 101 = 5050.\nThe sum of the first n natural numbers is n(n + 1)/2.\nThe sum of the first n odd numbers is n squared, and of the first n even numbers is n(n + 1).\nAlso t_n = S_n - S_(n-1), so we can find any term from the sum formula."
        }
      },
